{
  "dom_id": "step-q275580",
  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "A robotic arm randomly selects a box in a warehouse containing $n$ items from $1$ to $150,$ with each value equally likely. The arm then randomly picks one of these $n$ items and scans its position number (from $1$ to $n,$ each value equally likely).\n\nWhat is the expected position number scanned?\n\nThe expected position number scanned is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "This is a two-step random process: first, a box is selected containing $n$ items uniformly from $1$ through $150,$ and then an item is chosen uniformly in one of the positions numbered $1$ through $n.$\n\n- Let $Y$ be the number of items in the box. Since this is an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $150,$ we have $Y∼U\\{1,2,…,150\\}.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the position number scanned, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,150\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{150+1}{2}=75.5\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (75.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 76.5 \\\\  & =38.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
